  "strategy": "We propose to minimize the evaluation function J(a) = 2*n*max(convolve(a,a)) / sum(a)**2 using a Markov Chain Monte Carlo (MCMC) approach that explores the sequence space. The MCMC will generate proposal sequences by random walks and accept transitions based on the Metropolis-Hastings criterion, gradually concentrating the sequence distribution to the region that minimizes J(a).\n\nRationale: MCMC is particularly well-suited for the exploration of continuous, non-convex spaces that are common in optimization problems involving autocorrelation. Unlike deterministic optimization methods, MCMC can benefit from slow exploration, potentially escaping local optima that might trap other algorithms. This approach will include non-negative constraints (evaluation ensures validity) and allows for adaptive proposal distributions based on the current sequence state.\n\nSteps:\n1. Initialization: Start with the provided sequence (height_sequence_1 if available) or a uniform distribution (gradient renewal initialization).\n2. Proposal function: For each iterations, propose a new sequence a_new by adding scaled Gaussians to the current sequence.\n3. Acceptance criterion: Check if the objective function falls below an adaptive threshold based on the current best objective.\n4.Temperature schedule: Start with a high acceptance temperature and decrease it exponentially over time to speed up convergence.\n5. Adaptive cooling: Monitor progress and reduce temperature faster if the variance of new proposals drops too quickly.\nstrengths and Limitations: While MCMC can smooth out evaluation functions and find global optima, it requires careful tuning of hyperparameters to balance exploration and expiation. This includes adjusting the proposal function's scale and the temperature decay rate based on the average step improvement.\n\nWe further enhance the exploration by periodically reinitializing sequences in a high-dimensional noise layer (perturbation) if convergence appears stalled.\n\nFor sequential execution, we: debut the sequence of iterations, carry out temperature-based position adjustments, and adaptively update the proposal distribution with small perturbations in case of premature convergence.\n\nWe hope to achieve a J value lower than 1.5068, ideally approaching 1.5027.",
